Decisions:

```
if (score >= 80) {
  grade = "A";
} else if (score >= 65) {
  grade = "B";
} else {
  grade = "C";
}
const size = kg > 100 ? "big" : "small";     // one-line choice
```

`&&` and, `||` or, `!` not. Use `===`.

Loops:

```
for (let i = 1; i <= 12; i++) { ... }        // counting
while (saved < goal) { ... }                  // until something happens
for (const crop of crops) { ... }             // every item of an array or string
```

Watch out: `for (const x in list)` (with **in**) gives the *positions* as strings, not the items; `of` is almost always what you want. `break` leaves a loop, `continue` skips to the next round.

Loops that build a result: start an empty array (`const out = [];`), `out.push(...)` inside, return it after. Or a string, joined at the end: `lines.join("\n")`.

example.code:
function grade(score) {
  if (score < 0 || score > 100) return "invalid";
  if (score >= 80) return "A";
  if (score >= 65) return "B";
  return "C";
}
console.log(grade(85), grade(70), grade(30), grade(120));

const lines = [];
for (let i = 1; i <= 3; i++) lines.push(`7 x ${i} = ${7 * i}`);
console.log(lines.join("\n"));
for (const ch of "Habari") console.log(ch);

